# Dialog

A centred, blocking modal on a native `<dialog>` — for decisions the
user must make before continuing.

<%= embed :dialog, :default %>

## When to use

- A decision that blocks the flow: unsaved changes, required
  acknowledgement.
- A small, focused task that must complete before the page continues.

## When not to use

- **Confirming a deletion or other destructive action.** A dialog is
  dismissible — Escape, a backdrop click or the close button all close
  it *without answering the question*, so a stray click can dismiss the
  confirmation rather than record a deliberate choice. Destructive
  actions need an explicit, non-dismissable confirmation instead.
- Content consulted alongside the page — `sheet` (see the *Overlays*
  pattern page decision table).
- Action outcomes — `toast`. Page state — `alert`.
- Anything that grows into a multi-section form — give it a page.

## Usage

`render_dialog` (`Gesso::Components::DialogHelper`) renders the modal shell —
close button, title/description header — and yields the block as a
`<section>` body and `<footer>` actions (basecoat styles both as direct
children). Render it inside a `[data-controller="drawer"]` element
alongside the trigger that opens it.

```erb
<%%= tag.div data: { controller: "drawer" } do %>
  <%%= button_tag "Publish changes", class: "btn",
        data: { action: "click->drawer#open" } %>

  <%%= render_dialog(
        id: "publish",
        title: "Publish changes?",
        description: "The updated content becomes visible to everyone."
      ) do %>
    <%%= tag.section do %>
      Readers will see the new version immediately.
    <%% end %>

    <%%= tag.footer do %>
      <%%= button_tag "Cancel", class: "btn",
            data: { variant: "ghost", action: "click->drawer#close" } %>
      <%%= button_tag "Publish", class: "btn" %>
    <%% end %>
  <%% end %>
<%% end %>
```

| Param | Default | Notes |
| ----- | ------- | ----- |
| `id` | required | wires `aria-labelledby` / `aria-describedby` to the title and description |
| `title` | required | the decision, as a heading |
| `description` | — | supporting text under the title |
| `close_label` | `"Close"` | accessible label for the close button |
| `classes` | — | extra classes on the `<dialog>` |

Opened with `showModal()` via the drawer controller: focus trap,
Escape and backdrop dismissal are native. Long bodies scroll inside
the `<section>` (`dialog.css`); the dialog never exceeds the
viewport.

## How it opens

The trigger and the dialog are not linked by `id` — they are wired
through the `drawer` Stimulus controller by scope and targets. Wrap both
in one `data-controller="drawer"` element:

- **Trigger** — a button with `data-action="click->drawer#open"`. The
  controller's `open()` calls `showModal()` on its panel.
- **Panel** — `render_dialog` renders the `<dialog>` with
  `data-drawer-target="panel"`, so the controller knows which element to
  open. This target — not the `id` — is the link between button and
  dialog.
- **Close** — the built-in close button (and any Cancel you add) carries
  `data-action="click->drawer#close"`, which calls `close()`.
- **Backdrop** — the `<dialog>` carries
  `data-action="click->drawer#backdropClick"`; it closes only when the
  click lands on the dialog box itself (the backdrop), not its content.

`render_dialog` supplies the panel side; you supply the wrapper and
trigger. The same controller drives `render_sheet`. Because Escape and
the backdrop dismiss natively, the dialog is always closeable without
answering — see *When not to use*.

## Do and don't

- Do make `title` the decision ("Publish changes?") and the confirming
  button name the action ("Publish") — never "Are you sure?" + "OK"
  (see *Content*).
- Do keep the footer to primary + "Cancel"; a dialog with three
  choices is a design smell.
- Don't use a dialog to confirm a deletion or other destructive action
  — it is dismissible, so the confirmation can be closed without
  answering; use an explicit, non-dismissable confirmation instead.
- Don't open a dialog from a dialog — close the first (see
  *Overlays*: never stack).
- Don't open on page load or on a timer; a dialog answers a user
  action.

## Accessibility and content

- Native `showModal()` makes the page behind inert and traps focus;
  Escape, backdrop and the labelled close button all dismiss — three
  ways out, none custom.
- `title`/`description` are real heading + text in the dialog, read
  on open.
- Body copy carries only what the decision needs; detail belongs on
  the page underneath.
